const LX_C13_P1 = [
{title:"Radio interview with a geriatrician",intro:"You will hear part of a radio interview with Dr Verónica Salazar, a geriatrician in Loja who works with older patients in the valley of Vilcabamba.",voices:{H:V_.dan,G:V_.sar},
script:[["H","Good morning and welcome to Health Matters. The valley of Vilcabamba, in the south of Ecuador, has been famous for decades as a place where people supposedly live well beyond a hundred. My guest, Dr Verónica Salazar, is a geriatrician in Loja who has spent years working with older patients in the valley. Verónica, welcome."],["G","Thank you. It's lovely to be here."],["H","Geriatrics isn't the most glamorous specialism. What drew you to it?"],["G","Funnily enough, I was all set to become a cardiologist. Everyone in my year assumed that's where I'd end up, and I'd already done a placement in a heart unit in Quito. People also like to imagine there was some family drama behind it, because my grandfather had dementia, but he died when I was very small, so I barely remember him. What actually changed my mind was my rural year, in a health centre near Saraguro. Half my patients were over seventy, and I realised nobody had ever really listened to them. They'd been given pills for every complaint, often six or seven different ones, and no one had asked what they wanted from the years they had left. It was that, more than anything, that made me switch."],["H","Let's talk about Vilcabamba. Is it true that people there live to a hundred and twenty?"],["G","Sadly, no. In the seventies, researchers came from abroad and reported dozens of centenarians, and the story caught on all over the world. When the birth records were checked properly, it turned out many people had been adding years to their age, partly because being very old brought a certain respect. So the extreme numbers were exaggerated. Having said that, the older people I see there genuinely do age well. They're agile, they walk up and down those hills every day, and chronic disease seems to appear later than in the city. It isn't a Blue Zone in the official sense, and I'd be wary of anyone selling bottled Vilcabamba water as the secret, but it's still a remarkable place."],["H","So what is the secret, if there is one?"],["G","People always expect me to say diet. And yes, they eat in moderation: lots of vegetables, very little processed food. Genes play their part too, but, as far as the research is concerned, they account for only about a quarter of how long we live. Exercise matters, obviously. But if I had to choose one factor, it would be the fact that nobody there is ever really alone. Grandparents look after grandchildren, neighbours drop in, everyone has a role. Loneliness, frankly, is every bit as damaging to the cardiovascular system as smoking, and it's the one thing city life seems to multiply."],["H","The anti-ageing industry is enormous. What's your view of it?"],["G","Mixed, I'd say. There is serious science going on: clinical trials looking at whether certain drugs can decelerate cellular ageing, and I'd never dismiss that. What I object to is the shelf full of creams and supplements promising to rejuvenate your tissues or turn back your biological clock. Very few of them have been tested properly, and the ones that have rarely do what the label says. Patients spend money they don't have on them, and meanwhile they're skipping the walk that would actually help."],["H","And what's next for you?"],["G","I've been asked to write a book about Vilcabamba more than once, and I may do it when I retire. And no, I'm not opening a private clinic, whatever my colleagues say. What I'm starting in January is a training programme for family doctors in rural health centres, so that they can spot the early signs of dementia. Too many people in the countryside are diagnosed years late, when it's much harder to intervene. If we can catch it earlier, families have time to prepare."],["H","Dr Verónica Salazar, thank you."]],
q:[["What made Verónica decide to specialise in geriatrics?","her experience with older patients during her rural year","a placement in a heart unit in Quito","her grandfather's dementia","the expectations of her classmates"],["What does Verónica say about the claims made about Vilcabamba?","The ages were exaggerated, but older people there do age well.","The valley has been officially recognised as a Blue Zone.","Many residents there live to a hundred and twenty.","The local water explains the residents' long lives."],["Which factor does Verónica consider most important for a long life?","having strong social connections","eating in moderation","genetic inheritance","regular physical exercise"],["What is Verónica's main criticism of the anti-ageing industry?","Many of its products claim results that have not been proven.","Research into anti-ageing drugs should be stopped.","Its adverts are aimed unfairly at young people.","Its products are often dangerous to health."],["What is Verónica planning to do next?","train family doctors to detect dementia early","write a book about Vilcabamba","open a private clinic","carry out clinical trials in rural areas"]]},
{title:"Radio interview with an astrobiologist",intro:"You will hear part of a radio interview with Dr Mateo Rivas, an astrobiologist from Riobamba who has just spent two years working in the Atacama Desert in Chile.",voices:{H:V_.emm,G:V_.mic},
script:[["H","Welcome to Frontiers. The Atacama Desert in northern Chile is the driest non-polar place on Earth, and parts of it are so similar to Mars that space agencies test their rovers there. My guest, Dr Mateo Rivas, an astrobiologist from Riobamba, has just come back from two years at a research station in the desert. Mateo, welcome."],["G","Thanks. It's good to be back somewhere with a bit of humidity!"],["H","How did you end up in the Atacama?"],["G","I saw the job advert by chance. It was headlined 'Mars on Earth', and it asked for research assistants who were resilient, adaptable and ready to weather the storm of extreme conditions. Most people assume I'd always dreamt of space, and I did read a lot of science fiction as a kid, but I'd actually trained as a soil scientist, working on páramo soils around Chimborazo. What tipped the balance was my supervisor. She'd worked there herself and told me, quite bluntly, that if I didn't apply, she'd send the application for me. So I took a gamble."],["H","What does an astrobiologist actually do in a desert?"],["G","We study the limits of life. In the driest parts of the Atacama, it can go years without rain, and for a long time scientists assumed the soil was essentially sterile. What we look for are microbes that survive anyway, hiding inside salt crusts or just under the surface of rocks, where a tiny amount of moisture collects at night. If life can get through conditions like that here, it raises the fundamental question of whether something similar might once have existed on Mars. We're not looking for anything you could see without a microscope, I should add."],["H","What was the hardest part of living there?"],["G","People expect me to say the heat, and the days are blazing, but you get used to it. The nights are bone-chilling, which is almost worse, and there are no creature comforts, but neither of those bothered me much; I grew up camping. What really wore me down was the isolation. There were six of us, and in winter we could go three weeks without seeing anyone else. You learn a lot about yourself out there. I'd never thought of myself as someone who needs other people, and it turns out I very much am."],["H","Did you find anything surprising?"],["G","Yes. We expected the microbes to be dormant most of the time, switched off, as it were, waiting for water. In fact, some of them were active in the early morning, using the dew that formed on the rocks for only an hour or so. That's far less water than anyone thought life needed. It isn't proof of anything on Mars, of course, and I'd be careful with anyone who says it is. But it does widen the range of places where we might look."],["H","What's next?"],["G","I've had offers to go back to the Atacama, and I may well do so one day. There's also talk of a project in the dry valleys of Antarctica, which are about the only place even more extreme. But for now, I'm doing something closer to home. I'm setting up a small laboratory in Riobamba to compare the Atacama microbes with ones from the high, cold sandy areas around Chimborazo. It's a lot cheaper than Antarctica, and I can sleep in my own bed."],["H","Dr Mateo Rivas, thank you."]],
q:[["Why did Mateo apply for the job in the Atacama?","His supervisor strongly encouraged him to apply.","He had always dreamt of working in space science.","He had read about the desert in science fiction.","He wanted a change from soil science."],["What is the main aim of the research Mateo describes?","to find out how life survives in extremely dry conditions","to test rovers before they are sent to Mars","to search for water under the desert surface","to prove that life once existed on Mars"],["What did Mateo find most difficult about living in the desert?","being cut off from other people","the extreme heat during the day","the freezing nights","the lack of comfort at the station"],["What surprised the team about the microbes?","Some were active for a short time each morning.","They were dormant most of the time.","They needed more water than expected.","They could be seen without a microscope."],["What is Mateo going to do next?","set up a laboratory in Riobamba","return to the Atacama Desert","join a project in Antarctica","teach astrobiology at a university"]]},
{title:"Radio interview with a linguist",intro:"You will hear part of a radio interview with Dr Tom Ashworth, a linguist who studies words for emotions that are difficult to translate.",voices:{H:V_.hea,G:V_.lew},
script:[["H","Hello and welcome to Word for Word. German has Torschlusspanik, the anxiety that time is running out to seize life's opportunities. Japanese has age-otori, the disappointment of looking worse after a haircut. My guest, Dr Tom Ashworth, is a linguist who collects words like these, and he has spent the last three years in Ecuador working with Kichwa speakers. Tom, welcome."],["G","Thank you. Delighted to be here."],["H","Where does your fascination with these words come from?"],["G","People often assume it's because I grew up bilingual, but I didn't; I'm from a very monolingual town in Yorkshire. And I'd love to claim it began with some brilliant lecture at university, but I slept through most of those. It was actually my Portuguese flatmate. One evening she was looking at old photos and said she felt saudade, and when I asked what it meant, she spent an hour trying to explain it and finally gave up. Not being able to put a feeling into my own language really bothered me. I suppose I've been trying to catch up ever since."],["H","So if a language lacks a word for an emotion, does that mean its speakers don't feel it?"],["G","That's the question I get asked most, and the answer is almost certainly no. English speakers can feel the dread of missing out on life without having a word like Torschlusspanik. What a word does is make a feeling easier to notice and to talk about. It's a bit like having a label on a drawer: the contents were always there, but now you can find them quickly. So I'd say words shape how often we pay attention to an emotion, rather than whether it exists at all."],["H","What did you discover working with Kichwa?"],["G","Before I went, I expected the emotion vocabulary to be mostly about individual feelings, just as it is in English. What struck me instead was how many expressions describe feelings that belong to a group rather than a person. There's a sense in which joy or grief is something a family or a community has together. Mind you, I'm still learning the language myself, and I'd be very cautious about generalising. One speaker's explanation can be completely different from her neighbour's."],["H","Some people argue that emojis are becoming a universal language of emotion. Do you agree?"],["G","Not really. They're invaluable for gauging the mood of a message, and I use them constantly. But they're far from universal. The thumbs-up that I'd send to mean 'fine' comes across as rather cold to many younger people, and in some countries it's actually rude. The same face with tears can be joyful or full of despair, depending on who's sending it. So emojis don't remove misunderstandings; they just move them somewhere else."],["H","Your new book is about to come out. What's it about?"],["G","Everyone expects a dictionary of untranslatable words, and there are already plenty of those, some of them very good. Mine is more of a practical guide. It's about how people who live between two languages, migrants, students, mixed families, manage feelings they can only express in one of them. Several chapters are based on interviews with Ecuadorian families in Spain and in London. Honestly, those conversations were the most moving part of the whole project."],["H","Dr Tom Ashworth, thank you."]],
q:[["What first made Tom interested in emotion words?","a conversation with a flatmate","growing up speaking two languages","a lecture he attended at university","his first trip to Ecuador"],["What is Tom's view on languages that lack a word for an emotion?","Their speakers still feel it, but may notice it less.","Their speakers do not experience that emotion.","They eventually borrow the word from another language.","They express the emotion through gestures instead."],["What struck Tom about Kichwa expressions of emotion?","Many describe feelings shared by a group.","They are mostly about individual feelings.","All speakers explain them in the same way.","There are fewer of them than in English."],["What does Tom say about emojis?","They can still be interpreted in different ways.","They have become a universal language of emotion.","They are of little use for understanding a message.","Younger people use them more accurately."],["What is Tom's new book about?","how people living between two languages handle their feelings","a collection of untranslatable words","the emotion vocabulary of Kichwa","the history of emotion words in Europe"]]}
];

const LX_C13_P2 = [
{title:"Five people talk about nostalgia",topic:"nostalgia",who:["a music producer","a retired teacher","a university student","a psychologist","a shop owner","a photographer","a grandfather","a primary school teacher"],
op:["Nostalgia can stop us from enjoying the present.","Our memories of the past are often inaccurate.","Nostalgia is used to sell products.","Feeling nostalgic helps people cope with difficult times.","Young people can feel nostalgic for times they never lived through.","Old objects bring back memories more strongly than photos.","Living abroad made me appreciate my home culture.","Nostalgia brings different generations together."],
sp:[["My friends and I are obsessed with the nineties. We buy film cameras, we listen to cassettes in my cousin's car, we watch old episodes of Friends, all of which came out before any of us were born. My dad finds it hilarious; he says the nineties were mostly boring and the phones were terrible. He's probably right. But I think what we're missing isn't the decade itself. It's the idea of a time before everything was online, when you could make a mistake and nobody would post it. You can miss something you never had.",2,4,"nic"],
["I run a little shop in Cuenca selling sweets and toys, and I'll be honest, nostalgia is my best salesperson. Parents come in with their kids, spot the coconut sweets they ate at school, and suddenly they're buying three bags. The big brands know this too: every Christmas, the adverts on television are full of grandmothers, old kitchens and songs from the seventies. Some people say nostalgia unites families. Maybe it does. But from behind the counter, what I mostly see is that a happy memory loosens people's grip on their wallets.",4,2,"lew"],
["For a long time, nostalgia was treated almost as an illness; soldiers far from home were actually diagnosed with it. The research now tells a very different story. When people feel lonely, anxious or uncertain about the future, thinking back to meaningful moments tends to lift their mood and gives them a sense that their life has continuity. I saw it with patients during the pandemic, many of whom spent their evenings looking through old albums. It isn't about wanting to live in the past. It's a resource the mind uses to keep going when the present feels heavy.",3,3,"ada"],
["My granddaughter put all our family photos onto a computer, which was very kind of her, and I can look at them whenever I like. But they don't do much for me, if I'm honest. Last month I found my father's old radio in a cupboard, the one we used to listen to the football on in Riobamba. I turned the dial, and the click it made took me straight back to 1965: the smell of his tobacco, my mother shouting at us to come and eat. No photograph has ever done that to me.",6,5,"dan"],
["I photograph weddings, and I often meet the couples again ten years later to take family portraits. It's fascinating. They'll tell me their wedding day was perfect, the weather glorious, everyone happy. Then I show them the actual pictures, and it was pouring with rain, the bride's aunt looks furious and half the guests are soaked. They're always amazed. I don't think anyone's lying; the mind simply tidies things up. We remember the feeling and quietly delete the details that don't fit. So I take people's golden memories with a pinch of salt.",5,1,"isa"]]},
{title:"Five people talk about TV shows and values",topic:"television and values",who:["a screenwriter","a sociology lecturer","a teenager","a retired journalist","a parent","a film critic","a hospital nurse","an actor"],
op:["A TV series changed my opinion about a social issue.","Reality TV gives a distorted picture of ordinary life.","Recurring characters can feel like real friends.","Old series should be judged by the standards of their time.","Stereotypes on screen can harm real people.","Science fiction often predicts the future.","Watching series together helps families talk.","Television reflects society rather than changing it."],
sp:[["I'm not someone who believes television is ruining children; my parents said that about me, and I turned out fine. But I was worried that my teenage son and I had stopped talking. Then we started watching Stranger Things together on Friday nights. It's about a close-knit group of friends who stick together no matter what, and somehow, after each episode, we'd end up chatting about his own friends, about bullying, things he'd never have raised at dinner. The series itself didn't teach him anything. Watching it side by side just gave us a way in.",4,6,"hea"],
["I'm an actor from Esmeraldas, and for years the only parts I was offered were the same caricatures: the criminal, the security guard, the funny friend with no backstory. Casting directors would say, quite openly, that audiences expected it. What they didn't see was what happened afterwards. A cousin of mine was turned down for a job in Quito, and the manager actually joked about a character from a soap opera. That's when I realised these images don't stay on the screen. They follow people into job interviews and classrooms.",7,4,"fab"],
["My students love telling me that Friends made them more tolerant, or that Star Trek changed attitudes to race. It's a lovely idea, and I can see why it resonates with people. But when you look at the dates, progressive storylines nearly always appear after public opinion has already started shifting, not before. Producers are business people; they rarely take a gamble on something audiences aren't ready for. So I'd say television is a mirror, not an engine. It shows us what society has become, sometimes very powerfully, but it seldom leads the way.",1,7,"emm"],
["I'd always repeated what my uncle said about migrants, that they only come here to take our jobs. I'd never really questioned it. Then last year a friend recommended a series about a Venezuelan family trying to start again in Guayaquil. It wasn't preachy at all; it was funny, and the characters were multi-layered, with quirks and flaws like anyone else. By the end I felt completely differently. I even apologised to a girl in my class for something I'd said. My uncle hasn't watched it, unfortunately. I keep trying.",2,0,"sar"],
["I work night shifts on a hospital ward, and when I get home at eight in the morning, the house is empty. For years the same comedy has kept me company over breakfast. I know those characters better than some of my neighbours; I know exactly how one of them will react to bad news before he even opens his mouth. My sister thinks it's a bit sad, and perhaps it is. But when one of the actors died last year, I genuinely grieved, as though I'd lost someone I really knew.",6,2,"ali"]]},
{title:"Five people talk about inclusion in education",topic:"inclusion in education",who:["a head teacher","a wheelchair user","a university lecturer","a sign language interpreter","a rural teacher","a student from a working-class family","a policy adviser","a mother of a child with autism"],
op:["Small changes can make a big difference to inclusion.","Inclusion depends on teachers being properly trained.","Children should first be taught in their home language.","Separating students with special needs is sometimes justified.","Inclusion benefits all students, not only some.","Money alone will not make schools inclusive.","Social class still stands in the way of success.","Inclusive policies often exist only on paper."],
sp:[["I teach in a small community school in Chimborazo, where most of the children speak Kichwa at home. When I started, the official materials were all in Spanish, and the youngest pupils simply sat in silence, terrified of getting things wrong. Some colleagues said they needed to integrate as fast as possible. I disagree. Once I began teaching reading in Kichwa first, they came alive: they asked questions, they laughed, they argued. Their Spanish actually caught up faster afterwards. A child who can't understand the teacher isn't included, however welcoming the classroom looks.",4,2,"lew"],
["On paper, my son's school is a model of inclusion. There's a beautiful policy document on the website, full of phrases about valuing diversity and equipping every child with the tools needed to succeed. I asked for a quiet corner where he could go when the noise became too much, which would cost nothing. Six months later, nothing had happened. His teacher is lovely and well trained, and she tries hard, so I don't blame her. It's that the people who write the policies never seem to check whether anyone is following them.",7,7,"bel"],
["When I started at university in Latacunga, I expected the worst. There were stairs everywhere, and I'd heard that the lifts were permanently out of order. To be fair, they fixed the main lift quickly. But honestly, what transformed my experience wasn't some huge renovation. One lecturer simply moved our class to the ground floor, and another started uploading her slides before each lecture so that I didn't have to rush between buildings. Neither of those things cost a cent. They just required someone to think about it for a moment.",1,0,"mic"],
["Everyone at my school said that if you worked hard enough, you'd get in anywhere. I did work hard, and I did get into a good university in Quito. But in the first week I realised how far behind I was in things nobody teaches you. My classmates had done internships through family friends, they'd travelled, they knew how to talk to professors. My parents run a market stall; they'd never heard of half the words on the application form. Talent isn't the problem. It's the invisible advantages that some families can pass on and others can't.",5,6,"nic"],
["When we closed our separate unit and brought pupils with disabilities into ordinary classes, some parents were furious. They were convinced it would hold their own children back. Three years on, the results tell a different story. Not only have exam marks gone up slightly across the whole school, but the atmosphere has also changed. Pupils are kinder and more patient, and they're far better at working in mixed groups. I won't pretend it was easy, and a few staff were sceptical at first. But it's every child who has gained from it, not just a few.",0,4,"isa"]]},
{title:"Five people talk about water",topic:"water",who:["a farmer","a marine biologist","a city engineer","a fisherman","a hotel manager","a climate scientist","a resident of Guayaquil","an environmental activist"],
op:["Collecting rainwater has transformed my life.","Individual actions are only a drop in the ocean.","Floods are caused as much by planning as by rain.","Overfishing is a bigger threat than pollution.","Water should be treated as a human right, not a product.","People only value water when it runs out.","Technology alone cannot solve water scarcity.","Tourism puts pressure on water supplies."],
sp:[["Our farm is on the dry side of the mountains near Guano, and when the irrigation channel dried up three years ago, I honestly thought we'd have to sell. Then a project in the parish helped us put gutters on the roofs and build two big tanks. People laughed at first; they said a bit of rain from a roof was a drop in the ocean. But in the rainy season we now store enough to water the vegetables right through the dry months, and my children go to school instead of walking to the spring every morning.",0,0,"sar"],
["Every rainy season, my street in the south of Guayaquil turns into a river, and we lift the furniture onto bricks. People blame the heavy rain, or climate change, and they're not wrong. But my grandfather has lived here for fifty years, and he says it was never this bad. What changed is that the mangroves and wetlands nearby were filled in to build houses, and the drains were designed for a much smaller city. The water has nowhere to go. It's the planners' decisions, every bit as much as the weather, that leave us in deep water.",6,2,"eri"],
["I've fished off Santa Elena since I was a boy, and of course I see the plastic: bottles, bags, old nets floating everywhere. It's disgusting, and I'm not saying it doesn't matter. But that isn't what's emptying the sea. What's really doing the damage is the big industrial boats that come from far away and take everything, even the young fish that haven't had the chance to breed. Twenty years ago I'd come back with a full boat by midday. Now I'm lucky to fill half a box. There simply aren't enough fish left.",3,3,"dan"],
["We run a small hotel on the coast, and for years nobody gave water a second thought. Guests took half-hour showers, we watered the lawn at midday and the pool was refilled whenever we liked. And it wasn't only the guests; my own family was just as careless at home. Then, during the drought of 2024, the town's supply was cut for hours every day, and we had to buy water from a lorry at outrageous prices. Suddenly everyone became an expert in saving it. Most of us, I'm afraid, only realise what water is worth once the tap runs dry.",4,5,"emm"],
["Every few months a company announces a miracle machine that pulls drinking water out of the air or makes seawater cheap to drink, and journalists love it. Some of these inventions are genuinely clever, and desalination will certainly play a role on certain coasts. But they're expensive, they use enormous amounts of energy, and they don't touch the underlying problem: we waste and contaminate the water we already have. Without better management, fairer pricing and protection of the páramo that feeds our rivers, no gadget is going to get us back on track.",5,6,"ali"]]}
];

const CL_C13_1 = [
{title:"Set 1",ex:[
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about using emojis at work.",q:"Why has she stopped using emojis with her manager?",o:["He misunderstood one of her messages.","Her company introduced a new rule.","She thinks they look unprofessional."],a:0,lines:[["bel","I used to sprinkle emojis over every message, even at work. Plenty of people find that unprofessional, but our company's actually quite relaxed about it; there's no rule against it at all. Then I sent my manager a report with a little upside-down smiley, which to me means 'this was a nightmare, but it's done'. He read it as sarcasm, assumed I was unhappy with the project and called me into his office for a very awkward chat. Since then, it's words only with him."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about an anti-ageing cream.",q:"What does the man think about the cream?",o:["It is overpriced.","Its claims are not backed up by evidence.","It has irritated his skin."],a:1,lines:[["hea","You've been using that new cream for a month now. Any difference?"],["lew","My skin feels softer, I'll give it that. And it wasn't nearly as expensive as I'd feared."],["hea","So what's the problem? You look sceptical."],["lew","The box says it rejuvenates your cells and turns back your biological clock by ten years. I looked for the study, and there isn't one, just a survey of fifty customers. That's not science; that's marketing."],["hea","Fair point."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a film festival in Cuenca.",q:"What did he enjoy most?",o:["the conversations with directors","the quality of the films","the outdoor screenings"],a:2,lines:[["dan","I went to the film festival in Cuenca last week. The selection was a bit uneven, to be honest: a couple of brilliant documentaries and a lot of films that were ambitious but rather long. There were question-and-answer sessions with the directors after most screenings, which I'd been looking forward to, but they were so packed that I only got into one. What made the week for me were the evening screenings in Parque Calderón, with hundreds of people on blankets in front of the cathedral. That's what I'll remember."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a cross-country ski race in Greenland.",q:"Why did she nearly throw in the towel?",o:["Her equipment broke.","She was suffering from the cold.","She felt she was too slow."],a:2,lines:[["isa","The Arctic Circle Race is not for the faint-hearted. People imagine the cold is the worst part, and yes, it was minus twenty-five on the second morning, but with the right layers you're fine. One of my bindings came loose on the first day, though a mechanic sorted that out in five minutes. What nearly broke me was watching skier after skier overtake me on the long climb. I was convinced I was holding everyone up. If my partner hadn't skied beside me the whole way, I'd have thrown in the towel."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about his interview for a master's programme.",q:"What does he regret?",o:["not preparing questions for the panel","talking too much about his research","arriving late for the interview"],a:0,lines:[["mic","My interview for the master's went better than I'd expected. I'd been worried I'd get the time wrong, because the panel was in London and I was in Quito, but I logged in ten minutes early. They asked about my research on rural schools, and I think I explained it clearly without rambling. My only regret is the end. When they asked whether I had any questions for them, my mind went blank and I just said no. It must have looked as though I wasn't really interested."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a radio report about the weather in Guayaquil.",q:"What is the main purpose of the report?",o:["to explain the causes of a heatwave","to warn residents about possible flooding","to announce new drainage works"],a:1,lines:[["ali","Heavy rain is forecast for Guayaquil over the next forty-eight hours, following a week of record temperatures. The city authorities say that the new drainage channels in the north will not be completed until next year, so residents of low-lying areas in the south and west, particularly near the estuary, are advised to move valuables off the ground and to avoid driving through standing water. Emergency services have been placed on alert. Updates will follow on this station every hour."]]}]},
{title:"Set 2",ex:[
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a word in another language.",q:"Why does she like the word?",o:["It sounds beautiful.","It has no translation in any other language.","It describes a feeling she has often had."],a:2,lines:[["emm","My favourite word at the moment is the Italian rimpianto, which is regret for something you didn't do, as opposed to rimorso, regret for something you did. People keep telling me it's untranslatable, but that isn't quite true; you can explain it in a phrase. And no, it isn't the sound I love, although it is lovely. It's that I recognise myself in it. I spent my twenties turning down chances, a year in Chile, a band, and I've lived with exactly that feeling ever since."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about a TV series.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The main character is unrealistic.","The new season is longer than it needs to be.","The acting has improved since the last season."],a:1,lines:[["nic","Have you finished the new season of that political drama yet?"],["fab","Finally, last night. Ten episodes of an hour each. It could easily have been six."],["nic","Tell me about it. The middle bit dragged on forever. But I thought the prime minister was brilliant this time, far more convincing than before."],["fab","Really? I still find her a caricature. Nobody in politics is that cold."],["nic","Well, I've met a few who are!"]]},
{ctx:"You hear a geriatrician speaking on a radio programme.",q:"What is she doing?",o:["challenging a common belief","recommending a new treatment","describing her own experience"],a:0,lines:[["sar","A lot of my older patients are convinced that once you pass seventy, it's too late to change anything, so why bother? I understand the feeling, but the evidence says otherwise. Studies show that people who start strength training in their eighties can still make remarkable gains in a matter of months. There's no miracle pill involved, and you don't need expensive equipment either. A sturdy chair and a couple of bottles of water will do. It's never too late to make a difference."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about an expedition to Antarctica.",q:"What does he say about the expedition?",o:["The scientific results were disappointing.","Living together tested the team's patience.","The weather was worse than forecast."],a:1,lines:[["lew","We spent seven weeks crossing part of the Antarctic plateau to collect ice cores. Everyone asks about the cold and the storms, but the forecasts were remarkably accurate, so we were rarely caught out. And the samples we brought back are already giving the lab some fascinating data. What nobody prepares you for is spending fifty days in a tent with the same three people. By week four, the way one of them ate his porridge was driving me mad. We're still friends, just about."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a student talking about a gender studies course.",q:"Why did she choose the course?",o:["to prepare for a change of career","to question her own assumptions","to research a specific topic"],a:1,lines:[["hea","Everyone assumed I'd signed up for the gender studies module because I want to work for an NGO, which I don't, particularly; I'm doing engineering, and I'm staying in engineering. I don't have a research topic in mind yet either. Honestly, it was after an argument with my brother, when I realised I'd been repeating opinions I'd never examined. I wanted to find out where some of my own ideas about men and women actually came from. It's been uncomfortable at times, but in a good way."]]},
{ctx:"You hear an engineer talking about a rainwater project.",q:"What problem does he mention?",o:["The tanks are too small.","There is less rain than before.","Some families do not maintain their systems."],a:2,lines:[["ada","We installed rainwater harvesting systems in about forty homes in the hills above Cotacachi last year. Rainfall here is actually quite reliable, and the tanks store more than enough for a family's daily needs. The difficulty is maintenance. If the gutters aren't cleaned and the filter isn't changed, leaves and dust contaminate the water within weeks. A few households have been very careful, but others haven't touched theirs since we left. So this year we're training someone in each community to check them regularly."]]}]},
{title:"Set 3",ex:[
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a voice message he received.",q:"How did he feel when he listened to it?",o:["embarrassed","irritated","moved"],a:2,lines:[["fab","My mother isn't great with technology, so she's never sent me anything but the odd thumbs-up. Then on my birthday I got a four-minute voice message from her. I'll admit my first reaction was to sigh, because who listens to four minutes of anything? But it was her telling me about the day I was born, in the old hospital in Latacunga, and about the storm that night. I was standing in a supermarket queue in London trying very hard not to cry. I've saved it, of course."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two friends discussing a documentary about the Blue Zones.",q:"What does the woman think about the documentary?",o:["It ignored the role of genetics.","It made life in the Blue Zones look too easy.","It was too long."],a:1,lines:[["mic","Did you watch that documentary about the Blue Zones? I thought it was fascinating, all those centenarians in Okinawa and Sardinia."],["bel","The places were stunning, I'll give it that. And it did mention genes, to be fair."],["mic","So what didn't you like?"],["bel","It was all sunshine, laughing grandmothers and fresh vegetables. Nobody mentioned that many of those people worked in the fields from dawn to dusk for seventy years. That's hardly a lifestyle you can copy by buying a new cookbook."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a film director talking about her new film.",q:"What was her main aim?",o:["to capture everyday moments of family life","to criticise contemporary society","to make audiences laugh"],a:0,lines:[["isa","Critics keep describing my film as an attack on contemporary society, which rather surprises me. There are funny moments, I hope, but it isn't a comedy either. What I really wanted was to capture the mundane reality that films usually skip: a family arguing over the remote control, someone washing up in silence after a row, a grandfather falling asleep at the table. Those moments are where most of our lives actually happen, and I find them far more dramatic than any car chase."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a mountaineer talking about climbing Chimborazo.",q:"What was the biggest danger on the climb?",o:["altitude sickness","sudden storms","falling rocks"],a:2,lines:[["eri","People think the danger on Chimborazo is the altitude, and you do have to respect it, but we'd spent a week acclimatising, so none of us had more than a headache. The weather was kind to us, too; not a single storm. What really scared me was the rock fall. As the sun warms the face, stones break loose from the ice and come flying down the gully. On the way back, one the size of a football missed my partner by about a metre. That's when you realise you're taking your life in your hands."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman who interviews scholarship candidates.",q:"What advice does she give?",o:["Practise your answers aloud.","Find out about the interviewers beforehand.","Be honest about your weaknesses."],a:2,lines:[["nic","I've sat on scholarship panels for years, and I see the same mistake again and again. Candidates have clearly rehearsed in front of a mirror, which is fine, and some have even looked up our publications, which is flattering but unnecessary. Then we ask about a weakness, and they say they work too hard. We hear that ten times a day. What actually impresses us is someone who admits they struggle with something real, public speaking, statistics, whatever, and explains what they're doing about it."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a scientist talking about threats to the oceans.",q:"What point is she making?",o:["Plastic pollution has been exaggerated.","Marine heatwaves are becoming more frequent.","Fishing should be banned in more areas."],a:1,lines:[["hea","When people think about threats to the ocean, they picture plastic, and that's a real problem; I'm not downplaying it. Many people also call for fishing bans, which can work well in specific reserves. But what worries most of my colleagues right now is heat. Marine heatwaves, periods when the sea stays abnormally warm for weeks, are now roughly twice as frequent as they were in the early 1980s. Coral bleaches, fish move away and the whole food chain suffers. Around the Galápagos, we saw it clearly during the last El Niño."]]}]}
];

const CL_C13_2 = [
{title:"A talk about rainwater harvesting in the Andes",intro:"You will hear a water engineer called Rosa Quishpe giving a talk about collecting rainwater in highland communities in Ecuador.",voice:"isa",
text:"Good afternoon, everyone. My name is Rosa Quishpe, and I'm a water engineer working with rural communities in the province of Chimborazo. Today I'd like to tell you about a very old solution to a very modern problem: collecting rainwater.\nYou might think that a country with as many rivers as Ecuador would never run short of water. But in many highland communities, the reality is quite different. The rivers run deep in the valleys, far below the villages, and the springs that families have relied on for generations are drying up. Part of the reason is that the glaciers on Chimborazo have lost a large share of their ice in recent decades, and part of it is the damage done to the páramo, the high grassland that acts like a giant sponge, soaking up rain and releasing it slowly.\nIn some of the communities we work with, women and children used to spend up to three hours a day fetching water. That's time taken away from school, from farming and from rest.\nSo what does a rainwater system look like? It's surprisingly simple. Rain falls on a roof, usually made of corrugated metal, which is ideal because it's smooth and clean. Gutters carry the water to a pipe, and the pipe leads to a storage tank. The most important part, and the one people most often forget, is the first flush device. This is a small pipe that catches the first few litres of each storm, which carry dust, leaves and bird droppings off the roof, so that only cleaner water reaches the tank.\nFor a family of five, we usually install a tank that holds five thousand litres. In the rainy season, which here runs from roughly October to May, that fills several times over. The challenge is making it last through the dry months, so we also teach families to keep treated rainwater for drinking and cooking, and to use the rest for washing and for their animals.\nIs the water safe to drink? Not straight from the tank. We recommend either boiling it or using a simple ceramic filter. In sunny areas, families can also leave clear plastic bottles of water in the sun for six hours, which kills most bacteria.\nThe costs are modest. A complete system costs roughly the same as two months' income for a farming family, and in many cases the local parish council covers half.\nWhat has surprised me most, though, isn't the technology. It's what happens to the community. When people build tanks together, they start meeting regularly, they organise maintenance rotas, and in several villages those meetings have grown into cooperatives that now sell vegetables in Riobamba.\nOf course, rainwater harvesting isn't a magic solution. Compared with the scale of the water crisis, it's a drop in the ocean. But for a family that used to walk three hours a day, it changes everything.\nThank you very much.",
items:[["Rosa works with rural communities in the province of ___.",["Chimborazo"]],["The páramo acts like a giant ___, soaking up rain.",["sponge"]],["Some women and children used to spend up to ___ a day fetching water.",["three hours","3 hours"]],["Roofs made of ___ are ideal for collecting rain.",["corrugated metal"]],["The part people most often forget is the ___ device.",["first flush"]],["For a family of five, the tank usually holds ___.",["five thousand litres","5.000 litres","5000 litres","five thousand liters"]],["The rainy season runs from roughly October to ___.",["May"]],["Families can make the water safe with a simple ___ filter.",["ceramic"]],["In many cases, the local ___ covers half the cost.",["parish council"]],["In several villages, the meetings have grown into ___.",["cooperatives","co-operatives"]]]},
{title:"A talk about the Blue Zones",intro:"You will hear a researcher called Dr Andrew Collins giving a public talk about the Blue Zones, regions where people live unusually long lives.",voice:"mic",
text:"Good evening. I'm Dr Andrew Collins, and I research healthy ageing. Tonight I want to talk about the Blue Zones, a phrase you've probably seen on everything from cookbooks to breakfast cereals.\nThe term goes back to the early 2000s, when researchers studying longevity in Sardinia marked the villages with the most centenarians on a map with a blue pen. The name stuck. Later, the journalist Dan Buettner, working with demographers, identified five regions where people seemed to live unusually long and healthy lives: Sardinia in Italy, Okinawa in Japan, Ikaria in Greece, the Nicoya Peninsula in Costa Rica and a community of Seventh-day Adventists in Loma Linda, California.\nSo what do these places have in common? First, diet. People in the Blue Zones eat mainly plants, especially beans, and meat is kept for special occasions, eaten on average about five times a month. In Okinawa, older people repeat a saying before meals that reminds them to stop eating when they're eighty per cent full. It's moderation, rather than any superfood, that seems to be the key.\nSecond, movement. Interestingly, almost none of these centenarians go to a gym. Instead, they move naturally throughout the day: gardening, walking up hills, kneading bread. In Sardinia, many of the longest-lived men were shepherds who walked several kilometres a day over steep terrain.\nThird, purpose. The Okinawans have a word, ikigai, which roughly means a reason to get up in the morning. Some researchers believe that a sense of purpose may add up to seven years to life expectancy.\nAnd fourth, and perhaps most important, social connection. Older people in these places tend to live with or near their families, and they belong to tight groups of friends. In Okinawa, these groups, called moai, support each other financially and emotionally for life.\nNow, I should add a word of caution. In recent years, some researchers have questioned the data. In certain regions, birth records were incomplete or had been lost, and a few so-called supercentenarians turned out to be younger than they claimed. Okinawa, too, has changed. Since the arrival of fast food, it has had some of the highest rates of excess weight in Japan, particularly among younger generations.\nSo rather than treating the Blue Zones as a magic formula, I'd suggest seeing them as a collection of sensible habits. None of them is expensive, and none of them requires you to buy a product. As far as I'm concerned, that's precisely why they're so rarely advertised.\nThank you.",
items:[["The name Blue Zones comes from the ___ researchers used to mark a map.",["blue pen","pen"]],["One of the regions is the ___ Peninsula in Costa Rica.",["Nicoya"]],["In the Blue Zones, meat is eaten on average about ___ a month.",["five times","5 times"]],["In Okinawa, older people stop eating when they're ___ full.",["eighty per cent","80 per cent","80%","eighty percent","80 percent"]],["In Sardinia, many of the longest-lived men were ___.",["shepherds"]],["The Okinawan word ___ means a reason to get up in the morning.",["ikigai"]],["A sense of purpose may add up to ___ to life expectancy.",["seven years","7 years"]],["In Okinawa, groups of friends called ___ support each other for life.",["moai"]],["In certain regions, ___ were incomplete or had been lost.",["birth records"]],["Since the arrival of fast food, Okinawa has had high rates of ___.",["excess weight"]]]},
{title:"A talk about the Arctic Circle Race",intro:"You will hear a skier called Mark Henshaw giving a talk at a ski club about the Arctic Circle Race in Greenland.",voice:"dan",
text:"Good evening, and thanks for having me at the club. My name's Mark Henshaw. I've skied the Arctic Circle Race four times, and tonight I'll try to explain why anyone would choose to do something so obviously unpleasant.\nThe race takes place every spring around the town of Sisimiut, on the west coast of Greenland, just north of the Arctic Circle. It was first held in 1998, and it's often described as the toughest cross-country ski race in the world. Competitors cover around 160 kilometres over three days, across frozen lakes, steep hills and open plateaus.\nThe first thing people ask me about is the cold. Temperatures can drop to minus thirty, and with the wind it feels considerably colder than that. Race marshals check skiers' faces at every checkpoint, because frostbite is a real danger. But in my experience, the cold is something you can prepare for. What you can't prepare for is the weather changing in a matter of minutes. I once set off in bright sunshine and found myself in a whiteout within the hour, unable to see the skier in front of me.\nWhat makes this race different from most is the night between the first and second days. There are no hotels out there. Instead, every competitor sleeps in a tent at a camp in the middle of nowhere. The organisers provide the tents and a hot meal, but you have to carry your own sleeping bag and spare clothes. In my first year, I barely slept. I lay there listening to the wind and wondering whether the tent would still be standing in the morning.\nNot everyone finishes. Every year, a number of skiers throw in the towel, usually on the second day, which is the longest stage. There's no shame in that. Getting to the start line is already an achievement.\nPhysically, you need serious preparation. I'd recommend at least six months of training, with plenty of long, slow sessions to build endurance. But to be honest, the mental side matters even more. On the hardest climbs, it's your head, not your legs, that decides whether you keep going.\nSo why do it? Partly for the landscape. On a clear day, you can see icebergs out at sea from the top of the hills, and the silence is like nothing I've experienced anywhere else. Partly for the people. Local residents come out to cheer, and children wait at the finish line with flags. And partly, I suppose, because it's a humbling experience. You realise very quickly how small you are out there.\nIf any of you are thinking of taking part, my advice is simple: go with a friend, and don't try to win. Just try to get through it.\nThank you.",
items:[["The race takes place around the town of ___.",["Sisimiut"]],["The race was first held in ___.",["1998"]],["Competitors cover around ___ over three days.",["160 kilometres","160 km","160 kilometers","one hundred and sixty kilometres"]],["Race marshals check skiers' faces because ___ is a real danger.",["frostbite"]],["Mark once found himself in a ___ within an hour of setting off.",["whiteout"]],["Competitors have to carry their own ___ and spare clothes.",["sleeping bag"]],["Most skiers who give up do so on the second day, which is the ___ stage.",["longest"]],["Mark recommends at least ___ of training.",["six months","6 months"]],["On a clear day, you can see ___ out at sea.",["icebergs"]],["Children wait at the finish line with ___.",["flags"]]]},
{title:"A lecture about emojis",intro:"You will hear a researcher called Dr Laura Benítez giving a lecture to university students about the history and use of emojis.",voice:"bel",
text:"Hi, everyone. I'm Laura Benítez, and I research digital communication. Today we're looking at something you've probably used about thirty times before breakfast: the emoji.\nLet's begin with a bit of history. In 1982, a computer scientist called Scott Fahlman, at Carnegie Mellon University, suggested typing a colon, a hyphen and a bracket to show that a message was a joke. That little sideways smiley is known as an emoticon, and it caught on remarkably quickly.\nThe emoji itself came later, in Japan. In 1999, a designer called Shigetaka Kurita created a set of 176 tiny images for a mobile phone company. Each one was just twelve pixels by twelve. The word emoji, by the way, has nothing to do with the English word emotion. It comes from the Japanese for picture and character. Kurita's original set is now part of the collection at the Museum of Modern Art in New York.\nEmojis spread worldwide after 2010, when they were included in Unicode, the system that allows computers and phones to display the same characters. Five years later, Oxford Dictionaries chose the face with tears of joy as its word of the year, the first time a picture had ever been chosen.\nSo why have they become so popular? The main reason, according to most researchers, is that written messages lack the cues we rely on face to face: tone of voice, gestures, facial expressions. Emojis help to fill that gap. They let us gauge the mood of a message and soften criticism. A simple 'Can we talk?' can sound alarming; add a smile, and it's far less threatening.\nBut here's the catch. Emojis are far from universal. Studies comparing users in different countries have found that the same face can be interpreted in very different ways. The thumbs-up, for instance, is perfectly friendly for many older users, but a lot of younger people read it as passive-aggressive. And the skull, which once suggested death, is now widely used to mean 'I'm laughing so much I could die'.\nInterestingly, emojis are now turning up in courtrooms. Judges have had to decide whether a particular symbol counts as a threat, or even as agreement to a contract. In one case in Canada, a farmer who replied to a message with a thumbs-up was found to have accepted a deal.\nSo, are emojis making us worse at expressing ourselves? I'd argue the opposite. Used well, they add a layer of meaning that plain text lacks. The problem only arises when we assume everyone reads them the way we do.\nFor next week, I'd like you to keep a diary of every emoji you send over two days. We'll compare the results in class.\nThank you.",
items:[["Scott Fahlman suggested a sideways smiley to show that a message was a ___.",["joke"]],["The sideways smiley is known as an ___.",["emoticon"]],["Each of Kurita's images was just ___ pixels by twelve.",["twelve","12"]],["The word emoji comes from the Japanese for picture and ___.",["character"]],["Kurita's original set is now at the Museum of ___ in New York.",["Modern Art"]],["Emojis spread worldwide after they were included in ___.",["Unicode"]],["Oxford Dictionaries chose the face with ___ as its word of the year.",["tears of joy"]],["Many younger people read the thumbs-up as ___.",["passive-aggressive","passive aggressive"]],["In a case in ___, a farmer's thumbs-up was treated as accepting a deal.",["Canada"]],["Students have to keep a ___ of every emoji they send.",["diary"]]]}
];

const CL_C13_4 = [
{title:"Interview with an ultra-runner",intro:"You will hear an interview with Carla Montalvo, an ultra-runner from Latacunga who has raced in the Atacama Desert and in Antarctica.",voices:{I:V_.lew,G:V_.hea},
script:[["I","My guest today is Carla Montalvo, an ultra-runner from Latacunga who has raced across some of the most inhospitable terrain on Earth, from the Atacama Desert to Antarctica. Carla, welcome."],["G","Thank you. It's great to be here, and warm!"],["I","Let's start at the beginning. How did you get into extreme running?"],["G","Not in the way people expect. I wasn't sporty at school at all; I was the girl hiding at the back during PE. In my twenties, I was working in an office in Quito and smoking ten cigarettes a day. Then my father had a heart attack, and the doctor told the whole family, quite bluntly, that we had to change our habits. I started running round the park just to set him an example. I ran my first marathon two years later, and I suppose I simply kept going further."],["I","Your first ultra was in the Atacama. What was that like?"],["G","Two hundred and fifty kilometres over six days, carrying everything you need on your back apart from water. It was a humbling experience. I'd trained on the slopes of Cotopaxi, so I thought the altitude wouldn't trouble me, and it didn't. I'd prepared for the heat, too. What I hadn't counted on was my feet. By day three, they were so swollen and blistered that I could barely get my shoes on. I finished, but I spent the last stage in tears. I learnt more about looking after my body that week than in the previous five years."],["I","People often say that extreme athletes are dicing with death. Is that fair?"],["G","It's fair to say we take risks; there's no point pretending otherwise. But I don't think of myself as reckless. Before every race I spend months studying the route, the medical support, the evacuation plans. What frustrates me is when people assume we're adrenaline junkies who don't care what happens to us. Most of the athletes I know are incredibly careful. The ones who aren't don't last long in the sport."],["I","Then came Antarctica. Why there?"],["G","Honestly, because a journalist told me no Ecuadorian woman had done it, and I couldn't resist. The Antarctic Ice Marathon is run near the Union Glacier, a long way inland, at about minus twenty. The cold itself was manageable with the right clothing. What really got to me was the constant daylight. The sun never sets in the Antarctic summer, and my body simply didn't know when to sleep. I'd lie in the tent with an eye mask, completely exhausted and wide awake."],["I","How do you prepare mentally for something like that?"],["G","I used to think it was about being tough, gritting my teeth and refusing to think about the pain. Now I'd say the opposite. I break every race into tiny pieces: the next checkpoint, the next hill, sometimes the next hundred steps. And I talk to myself constantly, out loud, which other runners find hilarious. A sports psychologist taught me that, and it's made a bigger difference than any piece of equipment I've ever bought."],["I","You've also become involved in environmental campaigns. How did that happen?"],["G","Through running, really. When you spend days in places like the Atacama or Antarctica, you see how fragile they are. Then I'd come home and see the glacier on Cotopaxi shrinking every year. I'm not a scientist, and I don't pretend to be. What I can do is use the attention my races get. Now each race I run raises money for a project that protects the páramo above Latacunga, which supplies much of the town's water."],["I","What's next for you?"],["G","Everyone wants me to say Everest, and I have to admit it's tempting. But I don't want to queue behind two hundred people on a ridge. Instead, I'm planning to run the whole length of the Qhapaq Ñan through Ecuador, from the Colombian border to Peru, stopping at schools along the way to talk to children. It isn't the most extreme thing I've done, but it might be the most meaningful."],["I","Carla Montalvo, thank you."]],
q:[["What made Carla start running?","She had always been good at sport.","She wanted to encourage her father to live more healthily.","A doctor told her to take up running.",1],["What was Carla's biggest problem in the Atacama?","the high altitude","the heat","the condition of her feet",2],["How does Carla respond to the idea that extreme athletes are dicing with death?","She accepts there is risk but says most athletes are careful.","She thinks the risks are greatly exaggerated.","She admits that many athletes are reckless.",0],["What did Carla find hardest in Antarctica?","the low temperatures","sleeping in constant daylight","the distance from home",1],["How does Carla deal with the mental side of racing?","by ignoring the pain completely","by focusing on small parts of the race","by relying on the best equipment",1],["How does Carla support environmental causes?","by raising money through her races","by carrying out scientific research","by campaigning against tourism in Antarctica",0],["What is Carla planning to do next?","climb Everest","run through Ecuador and visit schools","return to the Antarctic Ice Marathon",1]]},
{title:"Interview with a film festival director",intro:"You will hear an interview with Esteban Proaño, the director of a film festival that takes place in Quito every October.",voices:{I:V_.sar,G:V_.fab},
script:[["I","My guest today is Esteban Proaño, director of the Andes Film Festival, which takes place in Quito every October and has grown from a weekend in a student cinema into one of the biggest events of its kind in the region. Esteban, welcome."],["G","Thanks very much. Delighted to be here."],["I","How did the festival begin?"],["G","Rather modestly. Twelve years ago, a few friends and I were frustrated that almost the only films showing in Quito were Hollywood blockbusters. We'd seen brilliant Ecuadorian and Bolivian films at festivals abroad that nobody here had ever heard of. So we borrowed a projector from the university, rented the student cinema for a weekend and hoped someone would turn up. About forty people came on the first night, half of them our relatives. My mother still claims she was the festival's very first audience member."],["I","What do you look for when you select a film?"],["G","People assume we're looking for big names, or for films that will win prizes. Neither is true, really. A famous director can make a dull film, and prize juries have their own fashions. What I look for is a film that haunts me. If I'm still thinking about a character a week after watching it, that's the one. Technical quality matters less than you'd think; some of our best films were shot entirely on phones."],["I","This year's theme is television. That's unusual for a film festival."],["G","It is, and some purists were horrified. But the line between cinema and television has almost disappeared. Many of the most ambitious directors are now working on series, because series give them time to build multi-layered characters with real backstories. In a two-hour film, the adversary is often little more than a caricature. In a series of ten hours, you can understand why someone became the way they are. We wanted to recognise that."],["I","Do you think TV shows actually shape people's values?"],["G","I'd love to say yes, given what I do. And there are examples where a series has clearly helped to shift public perception, of people with disabilities, for instance. But I'm cautious. Mostly, I think shows reflect values that are already emerging. What I'm more convinced of is that they shape what we talk about. A series can put a subject on every dinner table in the country overnight, and that's not nothing."],["I","What's been the biggest challenge in running the festival?"],["G","Funding, obviously, every single year. But that's true of every festival in the world. The hardest thing for us has actually been reaching people outside the north of Quito. For years, our audience was mostly young, middle-class and university-educated. So we started taking a mobile screen to parishes in the south of the city and to towns like Machachi and Sangolquí. The first time, I was worried nobody would come. In the end, we had three hundred people in a football stadium watching a documentary about potato farmers."],["I","Has the festival changed the local film industry?"],["G","To some extent. Several directors who showed their first short films with us have gone on to make features. And our industry day, when young filmmakers pitch their ideas to producers, has led to a few real deals. But I'd be careful not to exaggerate. Making films in Ecuador is still incredibly hard, and most directors here have a second job. The festival can't fix that on its own."],["I","Finally, what's your advice for someone going to a festival for the first time?"],["G","Don't only go to the films everyone's talking about. Pick at least one you know nothing about, from a country you've never visited. And stay for the questions afterwards. Hearing a director explain a single scene can change the way you see the whole film."]],
q:[["Why did Esteban and his friends start the festival?","They wanted to show films that were not available in Quito.","The university asked them to organise an event.","They wanted to promote their own films.",0],["What does Esteban look for when he selects films?","films by well-known directors","films with high technical quality","films that stay in his mind",2],["Why did the festival choose television as this year's theme?","Series are cheaper to show than films.","Series allow directors to develop more complex characters.","Audiences now prefer series to films.",1],["What is Esteban's view of the influence of TV shows?","They mainly affect what people talk about.","They are the main cause of changes in values.","They have little effect on society.",0],["What has been the festival's biggest challenge?","raising enough money","attracting audiences beyond its usual public","persuading directors to take part",1],["What does Esteban say about the festival's impact on the film industry?","It has solved many of the problems directors face.","It has helped some directors, but its impact is limited.","It has had no real effect.",1],["What does Esteban advise first-time festival visitors to do?","see only the most talked-about films","choose at least one unfamiliar film","skip the sessions after the films",1]]},
{title:"Interview with a university admissions tutor",intro:"You will hear an interview with Dr Rachel Morgan, the admissions tutor for a master's degree in Gender Studies at a college in London.",voices:{I:V_.dan,G:V_.bel},
script:[["I","My guest today is Dr Rachel Morgan, admissions tutor for the MA in Gender Studies at Baythorne College of Social Sciences. Every year she interviews dozens of applicants from all over the world, including a growing number from Latin America. Rachel, welcome."],["G","Thanks for having me."],["I","Let's start with the course itself. Who is it for?"],["G","A much wider range of people than you'd imagine. People tend to picture students who've all done sociology degrees, and some have, of course. But this year we've got a civil engineer from Ecuador, a nurse, a former police officer and a journalist. The course explores the complexities of identity in a global context, and someone who's worked on building sites or in a hospital brings experience that no textbook can give you. Prior study in the field is welcome, but it certainly isn't required."],["I","What do you look for in an application letter?"],["G","Not perfect English, I can tell you that. Many applicants worry about their grammar, and we're far more forgiving than they think. And I'm honestly not impressed by long lists of prizes. What I want to see is a real question: something the person has noticed in their own life or work and wants to understand. One applicant last year wrote about why there were no women on her engineering team in Latacunga, and whether that had anything to do with how girls are taught maths. That letter stood out immediately."],["I","And the interview? What do candidates get wrong?"],["G","The most common mistake is trying to guess what we want to hear. Candidates come in having memorised fashionable phrases, and they repeat them without really believing them. I'd much rather someone said, 'I'm not sure, but I think...', and then reasoned their way through it. We're not testing whether you already agree with us. Part of the course is learning to critically revise your own assumptions, and you can't do that if you're pretending to have none."],["I","Is the course itself inclusive? It's expensive, for a start."],["G","That's a fair point, and I won't pretend it isn't a problem. The fees are high, and living in London costs a fortune. We do have scholarships, and about a third of this year's students receive some financial support, but it isn't enough. Where I think we've made real progress is flexibility. Since we introduced a part-time option and recorded lectures, many more parents and people with full-time jobs have applied. Not all inclusion is about money."],["I","Some critics say gender studies is too theoretical to be useful. How do you respond?"],["G","I understand why people say that, because some of the reading is dense, no question. But our graduates go into very practical jobs: designing policies for city councils, working in human resources, running health programmes. The engineer I mentioned plans to go back and set up a mentoring scheme for female students at her old university. That's about as practical as it gets."],["I","What has changed most in your years as admissions tutor?"],["G","Probably the range of countries. When I started, nearly all our students came from Britain and the United States. Now more than half come from elsewhere: India, Nigeria, Brazil, Ecuador. That's made the seminars much richer and, frankly, much more challenging to run. Ideas that seem obvious in London get questioned very quickly by someone from a rural community in the Andes."],["I","Finally, what advice would you give someone thinking of applying?"],["G","Talk to a current student before you apply. Most universities can put you in touch with one. They'll tell you things the prospectus never will, like how much reading there really is."]],
q:[["What does Rachel say about the students on the course?","Most of them have studied sociology before.","They come from many different professional backgrounds.","They must have previous experience in the field.",1],["What impresses Rachel most in an application letter?","a genuine question based on personal experience","excellent written English","a list of academic prizes",0],["What is the most common mistake candidates make in interviews?","They are too uncertain about their answers.","They disagree with the interviewers.","They say what they think the interviewers want to hear.",2],["Where does Rachel think the course has made most progress on inclusion?","offering more scholarships","making the course more flexible","reducing the fees",1],["How does Rachel respond to the criticism that the subject is too theoretical?","She agrees that the reading is too difficult.","She points to the practical jobs that graduates do.","She says theory matters more than practice.",1],["What has changed most during Rachel's time as admissions tutor?","Students now come from a wider range of countries.","Seminars have become easier to run.","More students now come from Britain.",0],["What does Rachel advise future applicants to do?","read the prospectus carefully","speak to someone already on the course","prepare for a large amount of reading",1]]}
];
